# Testing

## Where tests live

- **Unit tests sit next to the code they test** (`foo.test.ts` beside
  `foo.ts`), run by `bun test` per package.
- **Each app owns its e2e suite** in an `e2e/` directory inside the app
  package (its own playwright.config.ts). There is no cross-app e2e package:
  the v1-v3 split proved that a shared test package for different apps has
  to be torn apart the moment the apps separate.
- **Shared test machinery lives in `qstd/testing`**: fixture builders,
  page-object bases, and generators (random images, deliberate duplicates,
  gibberish/corrupt/truncated files). Test helpers are deep modules held to
  the same house standards as production code — no copy-paste walls, house
  naming, one clear door per helper.

## What earns a test

- Test **behaviors and contracts**, never implementation details. A test
  that breaks on a refactor that preserved behavior is a defect in the test.
- Every test must earn its place: a suite is not better for being large.
  Before adding one, name the failure it would catch; if an existing test
  already catches it, don't add it.
- **Push tests down the pyramid.** If a unit test on pure logic (parsers,
  planners, reducers, date math) can prove the behavior, don't write an
  integration test for it; integration tests cover module seams and
  configuration; e2e covers only critical user flows end to end.
- **Combinatorics live at the integration layer**: enumerate failure modes
  (bad input, gibberish file, duplicate upload, canceled mid-flight,
  offline, quota) as a parameterized matrix over ONE well-built harness,
  not as hand-copied test bodies.
- Generated fixtures over checked-in blobs: build random/duplicate images
  and malformed files in code so cases are legible and infinite.
- A small number of **live tests** (real network, real backend) are allowed
  but tagged and excluded from the default run; they exist to catch what
  mocks cannot, not to re-run the whole suite against prod.

## The site map is test infrastructure

Each app maintains a committed site map (e.g. `e2e/site-map.ts`): every UI
surface, the route/interaction path that reaches it, and the stable handles
on it (roles, labels, test ids). Tests compose from the map instead of
rediscovering selectors, so writing a new test never starts with spelunking.
Build it as the UI is built, not after: when a feature lands, its surfaces
and handles land in the map in the same change, and implementations lead
with accessible names/test ids so there is always something stable to grab.
An exploratory pass that walks every page through the map doubles as the
smoke suite.

## Playwright discipline

- **Semantic locators only**: `getByRole`, `getByLabel`, `getByText`,
  `getByTestId` as the escape hatch. No CSS/XPath selectors that encode DOM
  structure.
- **Web-first assertions**: `await expect(locator).toBeVisible()` retries;
  `isVisible()`/`textContent()` in an assertion is a bug. Never
  `waitForTimeout` — if a wait is needed, something observable must be
  asserted instead.
- Tests are **isolated and parallel-safe**: fresh context per test, no
  ordering dependencies, each test creates (and owns) its data.
- Use aria snapshots (`toMatchAriaSnapshot`) for structure-level checks
  instead of piles of individual assertions.
- Keep traces/screenshots on failure on; investigate flakes, never mute or
  retry-loop them away.

## Accessibility and performance audits

Every UI-owning root has `bun run a11y` (axe-core against the dev server) and
`bun run lighthouse` (report written next to the root, gitignored). Run the
axe check as part of verifying any substantial UI change, with the dev server
already up; treat new violations like lint errors, not suggestions.
